This is the coding agent's whole `Program.cs`, as it runs in the terminal. It's the complete file, with its using lines:

```csharp
using LouisAgent.Core.Web;
using LouisAgent.Core.Anthropic;
using LouisAgent.Core.Ollama;
using LouisAgent.Core.OpenAi;
using LouisAgent.Core.Telemetry;
using LouisAgent.Core.Hosting;
using LouisAgent.Cli;
using LouisAgent.Coding;

// Usage: LouisAgent.CodingCli [prompt]. It loads what this demo's catalogue names.
await CliHost.RunAsync(args, CodingCatalog.Create(), new HostServices(WebAccess.Create, OtlpTelemetry.Instance, Providers: [AnthropicProvider.Instance, OllamaProvider.Instance, OpenAiCompatibleProvider.Instance]));
```

The last line is the whole program. `CliHost.RunAsync` takes the arguments, the catalogue and the host's services. With a prompt it answers once and stops. Without one it opens the chat. The catalogue is the only part that decides which agent you get. `CodingCatalog.Create()` names the coding agent's identities and packs. To start your own agent, you write your own catalogue and pass it in place of `CodingCatalog.Create()`. The docs I read don't show how to write a catalogue, so I can't give you that code from them. The build guides cover it.

Settings are read from the demo's `config/` folder, and the first file to set a name wins. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and `.env.secrets.example` to `.env.secrets`. Put `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in `.env.secrets`, or set `LLM_PROFILE=ollama` for a local model. Then run `dotnet run` from `demos/louis-agent/hosts/LouisAgent.CodingCli`.

Source: `src/docs/projects/LouisAgent.Cli.md`, the terminal host's design doc.
